While the Ruby code is already quite efficient, here are some ways to optimize the performance of the program using Scala:
1. Use a while loop instead of a for loop: In general, while loops are faster than for loops in Scala. So, you could replace the for loop with a while loop to improve performance.
2. Use tail recursion: Tail recursion is a technique where the last operation of a function is a recursive call. This allows the compiler to optimize the function and avoid creating a new stack frame for each recursive call. In the given example, you could use a tail-recursive function to print the numbers from 1 to 50.
3. Use lazy evaluation: In Scala, you can use lazy evaluation to delay the execution of a computation until it is actually needed. This can help improve performance by avoiding unnecessary computations. For example, you could use a lazy sequence to generate the numbers from 1 to 50.
Here is an optimized version of the program using a while loop:
