To correct grammatical errors in a sentence using Java, you can use Natural Language Processing (NLP) libraries such as Stanford CoreNLP, Apache OpenNLP, or LingPipe. These libraries provide various features for text analysis, including tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, dependency parsing, and named entity recognition.
For your specific task of correcting a grammatical error in a sentence, you can use the dependency parsing feature to identify the relationship between words in the sentence and detect errors such as missing or misplaced words.
Here's a code snippet using Stanford CoreNLP to correct the sentence "The cat eating the fish":
```java
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.*;
import java.util.*;
public class GrammarCorrection {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // set up pipeline
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, parse");
    StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
    // input sentence
    String sentence = "The cat eating the fish";
    // create annotation object
    Annotation document = new Annotation(sentence);
    pipeline.annotate(document);
    // get the first sentence's parse tree
    List<CoreMap> sentences = document.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
    Tree tree = sentences.get(0).get(TreeCoreAnnotations.TreeAnnotation.class);
    // correct the error
    String correctedSentence = tree.toString().replaceAll("VBG", "VBD");
    // output corrected sentence
    System.out.println(correctedSentence);
  }
}
```
In this example, we use Stanford CoreNLP to parse the input sentence and obtain its parse tree. We then correct the error by replacing the present participle verb form ("VBG") with the past tense verb form ("VBD") using a regular expression. Finally, we output the corrected sentence.
Note that this is a simple example and there are many other types of grammatical errors that can be corrected using NLP techniques.

