Yes, we can use natural language processing techniques in Swift to classify the given article as either sports or business. We can extract relevant keywords and phrases related to sports and business using techniques such as tokenization, stemming, and part-of-speech tagging. We can then use these features to train a machine learning model for classification.
To consider the sentiment expressed in the announcement made by The New York Yankees and Logitech, we can use techniques such as sentiment analysis to classify the article as positive, negative, or neutral. This can be used to further refine our classification approach.
Here is some sample Swift code for implementing this task:
```
import NaturalLanguage
// Sample article text
let articleText = "The New York Yankees announced a partnership with Logitech to improve their video analysis capabilities. The deal is expected to boost the team's performance on the field."
// Tokenize the article text
let tokenizer = NLTokenizer(unit: .word)
tokenizer.string = articleText
let tokens = tokenizer.tokens(for: articleText.startIndex..<articleText.endIndex)
// Perform part-of-speech tagging on the tokens
let tagger = NLTagger(tagSchemes: [.lexicalClass])
tagger.string = articleText
let tags = tokens.map { token in
    tagger.tag(at: articleText.index(token.startIndex, offsetBy: token.utf16Offset(in: articleText)), unit: .word, scheme: .lexicalClass, options: [])
}
// Extract relevant keywords and phrases
let sportsKeywords = ["baseball", "football", "basketball", "hockey"]
let businessKeywords = ["partnership", "deal", "performance", "boost"]
var sportsCount = 0
var businessCount = 0
for (index, tag) in tags.enumerated() {
    if sportsKeywords.contains(tokens[index].lowercased()) {
        sportsCount += 1
    } else if businessKeywords.contains(tokens[index].lowercased()) {
        businessCount += 1
    }
}
// Classify the article based on keyword counts
if sportsCount > businessCount {
    print("This article is about sports.")
} else {
    print("This article is about business.")
}
