% Set initial values
job_seekers = 1000;
job_openings = 500;
employed = 500;
unemployed = job_seekers - employed;
unemployment_rate = unemployed / (employed + unemployed);
% Set simulation parameters
num_years = 10;
time = 1:num_years;
% Simulate job market over time
for t = 1:num_years
    % Update job seekers and job openings
    job_seekers = job_seekers - round(job_seekers * 0.05); % 5% of job seekers find employment each year
    job_openings = job_openings + round(job_openings * 0.03); % 3% more job openings become available each year

    % Calculate new employment and unemployment levels
    employed = min(job_seekers, job_openings);
    unemployed = job_seekers - employed;
    unemployment_rate(t+1) = unemployed / (employed + unemployed);
end
% Plot results
figure;
subplot(2,1,1);
plot(time, job_seekers, time, job_openings);
xlabel('Time (years)');
ylabel('Number of individuals');
legend('Job seekers', 'Job openings');
title('Trends in job seekers and job openings over time');
subplot(2,1,2);
plot(time, unemployment_rate(2:end));
xlabel('Time (years)');
ylabel('Unemployment rate');
title('Trends in unemployment rate over time');
```
This code sets initial values for job seekers, job openings, and employment levels, and then simulates the job market over a specified number of years. The simulation updates the number of job seekers and job openings each year, and calculates new employment and unemployment levels based on these values. The unemployment rate is then calculated and stored for each year.
